  "live_records": {
    "q-133235": {
      "dom_id": "step-q133235",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b$, if \n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(x,y)=2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+a{y}^{2},\\,{f}_{y}(x,y)=bx\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+6xy.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the mixed partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+a{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-2yx\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+2ay \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(bx\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+6xy) \\\\  & =b\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-bxy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+6y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. Hence, we have the identity\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-2yx\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+2ay & =b\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-bxy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+6y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $y$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy),$ respectively, we see that $a=3$ and $b=2.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
